WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.421
With Rambo: Last Blood, Sylvester Stallone
hopes to put an exclamation point at the end

00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:07.020
of the five-film series that is John Rambo's
story.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.720
Last Blood follows Rambo's struggle to get
his niece Gabrielle back from a Mexican cartel

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.960
after they abduct her for a human trafficking
ring.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.100
Viewers know to expect a bloody confrontation,
but that doesn't mean the ending is entirely

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:18.680
predictable.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.860
With that in mind, let's dig into the end
of Rambo: Last Blood.

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:23.560
Warning: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:28.130
John Rambo began his cinematic journey in
First Blood as a man who was just trying to

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.820
find a way to move on in a country that didn't
seem to have a place for him anymore.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.340
In the four films since then, Rambo has battled
within himself to quell a warrior's rage while

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.820
also shooting lots of people.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.140
Peace just doesn't seem to be an option for
Rambo.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.070
Stallone said in an interview with Variety:

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.280
"You can totally end the story with him going
home, having that shot of him going down the

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:47.739
driveway, which is completion.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.900
But a character like that - does he ever really
go home?

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:54.480
I jotted down on a Post-It: 'He came home,
but he never arrived,' and I went, there's

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.020
a movie here.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.820
The warrior can never find peace.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:57.960
He just can't."

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.880
That sense of never truly feeling at peace
physically manifests in Last Blood as the

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.040
tunnels Rambo digs.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.650
It's also encapsulated in something he tells
Gabrielle early on: He never changed, he just

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.840
learned to "keep a lid on it."

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.220
Through the ultra-violent ending of the film,
we see that the lid wasn't on too tight.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.220
"I want revenge."

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.640
A hallmark of the Rambo franchise has always
been the appearance of a somewhat hopeful

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.670
figure in John Rambo's life right when he's
at his most hopeless.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.060
That was his mentor Col. Trautman in the first
film, and in recent films, it's taken the

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.200
form of a woman hoping to convince Rambo to
move on with his life.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.180
In Last Blood, that's Carmen, a journalist
covering Mexican cartels who lost her sister

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.800
to their human trafficking machine.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.220
Carmen cleans John up when the Martinez brothers
beat and scar him, then helps him find the

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.560
right connections to exact vengeance later.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.040
With Rambo's quest for blood finished, what
happens to Carmen?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.399
There's no way she wouldn't hear about his
one-man defeat of the Martinez cartel and

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:53.659
all of their enforcers, and it's hard to imagine
she'd just be a bystander after forming a

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:54.640
bond with Rambo.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.080
It's quite possible that the aftermath of
the film creates an opportunity for her to

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.720
step in and tell the world the story of what
happened that day on the Rambo family ranch.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.380
Rambo's blood relatives have all died since
he returned to America, but he does have a

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.220
chosen family: His "niece" Gabrielle and her
grandmother Maria, who looks after the Rambo

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.300
family estate and takes care of Gabrielle.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.780
The tragic loss of Gabrielle to a cartel-driven
drug overdose is the emotional linchpin of

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.940
Last Blood, and drives Rambo to action.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.010
Before that happens, though, Maria leaves
the Rambo family ranch behind, taking her

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.660
memories of Gabrielle with her.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.010
So what happens to Maria?

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:31.320
It seems likely that she's the last living
person Rambo would leave any kind of inheritance

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.020
to, which would place her at the center of
his story as the public learns of his war

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:35.640
with the cartel.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.800
But it's unclear whether she'll choose to
return.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.700
Rambo: Last Blood is a human story about a
man who feels there's nothing he can do but

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:43.200
fight.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.380
"Something happened that's traumatic."

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.280
"He's pushed."

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.340
But it's also a story in which a couple dozen
members of a Mexican cartel come to Arizona

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.170
with assault rifles to kill a guy who just
happened to have enough firepower to bring

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.160
them all down and blow up half his property
while he's at it.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.430
Yes, it's a Rambo movie, but it still exists
in a version of our world, so there are bound

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.280
to be some political implications stemming
from Rambo's last stand against the cartel.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.240
At one point, we see cartel members showing
up to a crime scene and walking right through

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.460
the police with their guns drawn.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.260
These guys can seemingly do whatever they
want with impunity.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.140
After the battle in Arizona, the cartels might
not go away, but a greater public focus on

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.940
human trafficking could lead to some kind
of crackdown, likely at the border.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.340
So what actually happens to John Rambo?

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.620
As the film ends, Rambo has just won his battle
with the cartel, literally cutting out Hugo

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:29.800
Martinez's heart.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:34.300
He stumbles onto his porch to sit in a rocking
chair and nurse the gunshot wound in his abdomen.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.420
As he does, the camera zooms out, and we see
the chair is still rocking as the credits

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:38.920
roll.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:39.700
Is he alive?

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.300
Rambo's ending monologue about cherishing
the memories of those he's lost is followed

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.800
by a credit montage of moments from all four
previous films.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.580
This culminates in a wounded, bloody Rambo
climbing on a horse and riding away from the

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.080
ranch.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.720
But the film made a point of showing us that
Rambo drove his horses away from the barn

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.340
to protect them from the cartel.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:00.160
Did this horse wander back, or is that Rambo's
passage into the next life in the form of

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.380
a ride into the sunset?

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.340
It's certainly up for interpretation.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.330
From First Blood on, the overarching narrative
of the Rambo series is violence begetting

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.330
violence.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.930
Rambo is a warrior because he's good at it
and he was trained to be, but he doesn't start

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.420
the fight.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.180
"They drew first blood, not me."

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.440
He's pushed to the edge, and then he pushes
back.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.840
Then the cycle continues even as he tries
to keep a lid on it.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.770
In Last Blood, this sense of being pushed
too far culminates in his explanation that

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.160
he can never stop as long as innocent people
are being harmed.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.280
There's an undercurrent of hope at the end
of Last Blood, though, as Rambo's parting

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.680
words to the audience meditate on memory and
loss.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.440
He doesn't want other people to be like him.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.260
He doesn't want people to get hurt and suffer
senselessly.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.250
He just knows he has something inside him
that will react when injustice does happen,

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.340
though by the end of the film he seems to
have found a way to make peace with that through

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:45.840
memory.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.340
If he can draw last blood, maybe it will finally
be enough.

